[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 2, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of the CaLMFlow framework. CaLMFlow takes as input textual conditions and flows and generates the next time point for the flows. The textual condition is tokenized and embedded using the LLM tokenizer and embedding layer while the conditional flows are transformed into spatial-temporal tokens using a learned projection. If multiple conditional flows are input simultaneously, the tokens are ordered by flow, space, and then time. The LLM applies causal language modeling and generates the next time point for each flow.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow diagram for generating conditional flows with a tokenized representation and an LLM-based next-token prediction model.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- **Input Conditional Flows**: Shows “Source distribution (t=0)” and “Target distribution (t=1)” with several 2D point-cloud distributions. Conditions are labeled as **Textual condition**, **Cell type**, and **Image label**. Example image-label panels include small image patches resembling digits or microscopy-like images.\n- **Tokenized Flow Tensor**: Shows a 3D tensor/grid labeled with axes including **Time (flow)** and trajectory-related dimensions. A highlighted token is labeled with components resembling `(t0, x0, s0)`, and the tensor is described as “Sequentialize” into token sequences.\n- **Next Token Prediction**: Shows an autoregressive sequence modeling setup with the label **Volterra IE** and an equation form for predicting `z_t` from prior state and an integral term. A central block labeled **LLM** receives condition tokens and flow tokens at times `t=0 ... t=T`. A lower block labeled **VAE Decoder** outputs parameters labeled `(μ, σ)`.\n- **Generated Flows**: Shows generated 2D point-cloud distributions at multiple time points labeled **t=0**, **t=0.1**, **t=0.2**, ellipsis, and **t=1**, illustrating evolution from initial to final distributions.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No explicit biological specimen, tissue, cell image, or molecular object is shown. The “Cell type” label appears as a conditioning category, but the plotted objects are abstract colored point clouds.\n\nTransformations and model interfaces:\n- Conditional input distributions are converted into a **tokenized flow tensor**.\n- The tensor is sequentialized into token sequences.\n- Condition tokens and flow tokens are passed into an **LLM** for next-token prediction.\n- A **VAE Decoder** appears to decode predicted latent outputs into distribution parameters.\n- The final output is a sequence of generated flow states over time.\n\nFindings shown:\n- The figure communicates a method pipeline, not experimental results. It visually indicates that conditional source distributions can be tokenized, modeled autoregressively, and decoded to generate time-evolving flows toward target distributions."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 2: Heatmaps of the ground truth 4 Gaussians dataset (2a) and that generated by CFM (2b), CaLMFlow (1 traj.) (2c), and CaLMFlow (8 traj.) (2d). Both variants of CaLMFlow generate a distribution that closely matches the ground truth, with the 8-trajectory version further enhancing performance by distributing the data more evenly and accurately.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with four side-by-side heatmap/scatter-density panels labeled:\n\n- **(a) Ground Truth**: A square field with four compact high-density clusters near the corners on a dark purple background.\n- **(b) CFM**: A square field with many dispersed points forming a broad, noisy central cloud, with no clear corner-cluster structure.\n- **(c) CaLMFlow (1 traj.)**: A square field showing partially recovered clustered structure, with several diffuse groups including visible concentrations near upper-left, upper-right, lower-left, and right/lower-right regions.\n- **(d) CaLMFlow (8 traj.)**: A square field closely resembling the ground truth, with compact clusters near the four corners and sparse background noise.\n\nVisible content appears to compare generative or flow-based model outputs against a ground-truth distribution. The biological source objects are not identifiable from the image alone; it shows abstract spatial distributions/heatmaps rather than recognizable cells, tissues, proteins, or microscopy objects.\n\nMain visible finding: **CaLMFlow with 8 trajectories better matches the four-cluster ground-truth distribution than CFM or CaLMFlow with 1 trajectory**, which appear noisier or less structured."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 3: Comparison of conditional generation quality across different models for single-cell perturbation data. CaLMFlow (3a and 3b) exhibits strong overlap between generated data distribution (blue) and the ground-truth distribution (orange), highlighting its superior capability to model data with unseen combinatorial perturbations. In contrast, other models struggle to produce realistic samples. For CaLMFlow, R.I. refers to randomly initialized CLM, and N.L. refers to natural language pretrained CLM.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with 8 scatterplot panels arranged in a 2 x 4 grid. Each panel compares two point clouds labeled in the legend as blue “Generated” and orange “Original.”\n\nVisible panel labels:\n- (a) CaLMFlow (R.I.)\n- (b) CaLMFlow (N.L.)\n- (c) CFM\n- (d) CFM-OT\n- (e) CFM-SB\n- (f) scVI\n- (g) scGPT\n- (h) CPA\n\nContent and structure:\n- Each panel shows a 2D embedding or projection of data points.\n- Blue points represent generated samples.\n- Orange points represent original samples.\n- The figure visually compares how well different methods reproduce the distributional structure of the original data.\n- Some methods show stronger overlap between generated and original distributions, while others show separated clusters, curved trajectories, or mismatched point-cloud shapes.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The panel names include single-cell-related models or methods such as scVI, scGPT, and CPA, suggesting the plotted objects are likely single-cell data embeddings or generated vs original cellular profiles.\n- No cell types, tissue sources, genes, species, or experimental conditions are visible in the extracted image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure compares outputs from multiple generative or flow-based modeling methods: CaLMFlow variants, CFM variants, scVI, scGPT, and CPA.\n- The visible transformation is from original data distribution to generated data distribution, evaluated in a shared 2D scatterplot space.\n\nVisible finding:\n- CaLMFlow panels appear to show substantial overlap between generated and original point clouds.\n- Several baseline methods show clearer distribution mismatch, including separated clusters or poorly aligned trajectories."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 10, "caption": "Figure 4: Ablation results on temperature. Left : CaLMFlow generated data from 8gaussians to 2moons, using different temperature values. Right : 2-Wasserstein and MMD performances as a function of temperature. The plots show that a low, non-zero temperature value ( τ =0.2) produces the best performance and that the VAE is necessary.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about point-cloud/domain transformation behavior with quantitative metrics.\n\nVisible content:\n- Left block: six scatter-plot panels arranged in a 2 x 3 grid.\n- Panel labels:\n  - “Ground Truth”\n  - “No VAE”\n  - “t=0”\n  - “τ=0.2”\n  - “τ=1”\n  - “τ=2”\n- Legend in the Ground Truth panel:\n  - red points: “source”\n  - blue points: “target”\n- The scatter plots show red and blue 2D point clouds. The ground-truth panel has red source clusters distributed around a blue spiral-like target structure. Other panels show progressively transformed or generated point distributions at different temperature values τ.\n- The “No VAE” panel shows a compact blue cluster and surrounding red clusters.\n- The τ panels show blue target-like point clouds becoming more diffuse as τ increases, with red source clusters remaining around or mixed into the space.\n\nRight block:\n- A line plot with x-axis labeled “Temperature (τ)”.\n- Left y-axis in red labeled “2-Wasserstein”.\n- Right y-axis in blue labeled “MMD”.\n- Red and blue curves with shaded confidence bands.\n- Both metrics drop sharply at low τ, then increase steadily as τ approaches 3.\n- The plotted finding appears to be that intermediate/low temperature minimizes both 2-Wasserstein distance and MMD, while higher temperatures worsen both metrics.\n\nNo biological source objects are visible. The figure appears to use synthetic 2D point-cloud data rather than microscopy, anatomical, cellular, molecular, or organismal imagery."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 16, "caption": "Figure 5: Comparison of the ground truth data and model-generated data, colored by Leiden labels. For the generated data, the Leiden labels are predicted by an MLP classifier trained on the ground truth. Both variants of CaLMFlow successfully generate data spanning all clusters and closely align with the ground truth distribution. In contrast, while CFM, CFM-OT, and CFM-SB generate data across all classes, they fail to differentiate between them, indicating a mismatch in the underlying community structures. Models such as scVI, scGPT, and CPA are unable to generate data for some classes altogether.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with nine labeled scatter/embedding plots arranged in a 3x3 grid. Each panel shows colored point clouds with class labels in a legend, likely comparing learned representations or transformations across methods.\n\nVisible panels:\n- (a) Ground Truth: separated colored clusters labeled 0-7.\n- (b) CaLMFlow (R.I.): mixed embedding with several colored groups and separated purple/brown structures.\n- (c) CaLMFlow (N.L.): large mixed oval-shaped point cloud with additional side clusters.\n- (d) CFM: two mostly separated vertical structures, dominated by brown on the left and green/gray on the right.\n- (e) CFM-OT: dense mixed cluster at lower left with elongated trajectories/structures extending upward and right.\n- (f) CFM-SB: dense mixed cluster at lower left with large curved purple trajectory and green/gray extensions.\n- (g) scVI: sparse points, mostly orange, forming a curved arc; legend shows only classes 3 and 7.\n- (h) scGPT: two vertical orange-dominated point clouds; legend shows classes 0 and 7.\n- (i) CPA: multiple curved trajectory-like colored structures; legend includes labels 0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The figure appears to visualize single-cell data embeddings or generated/translated cell-state distributions, based on method names such as scVI, scGPT, and CPA. No specific organism, tissue, cell type, gene, or assay is visible in the image.\n\nModel/method interfaces:\n- Compared methods include CaLMFlow variants, CFM variants, scVI, scGPT, and CPA.\n- The visual output interface is 2D scatter plots with categorical color labels.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Ground truth shows well-separated clusters, while different methods produce varying degrees of class mixing, separation, collapse, or trajectory-like structures. CaLMFlow, CFM variants, scVI, scGPT, and CPA differ substantially in how closely their colored point distributions resemble the ground-truth cluster structure."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 19, "caption": "Figure 6: Comparison of UMAPs of generated versus ground truth single-cell data between CFM and CaLMFlow. For each model, we generated 20,000 cells and plotted a UMAP with 20,000 randomly sampled cells from the immune cell dataset from Dong et al. (2023). The plots demonstrate that CaLMFlow produces a single-cell distribution more accurately reflecting the ground truth data, in contrast to CFM.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains a two-panel scientific visualization comparing point-cloud or embedding distributions of generated versus original samples.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel labeled “(a) CFM”\n- Right panel labeled “(b) CaLMFlow”\n\nLegend:\n- Blue points: “Generated”\n- Orange points: “Original”\n\nContent:\n- Each panel shows overlaid 2D scatter distributions of generated and original data.\n- In panel (a), the blue and orange point clouds largely overlap in a dominant elongated central structure, with a few small separated clusters or outliers.\n- In panel (b), the distributions are more fragmented into multiple separated clusters, with partial overlap between blue and orange points across several regions.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No explicit biological objects, cell types, tissues, organisms, or microscopy structures are visible in the extracted image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The panels compare outputs from two models or methods: CFM and CaLMFlow.\n- The visible transformation appears to be a low-dimensional projection or embedding used to compare generated samples against original samples.\n\nFindings shown:\n- CFM appears to produce generated points that closely follow the main original distribution.\n- CaLMFlow shows generated and original points distributed across multiple clusters with noisier or less continuous overlap."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 19, "caption": "Figure 7: Comparison of conditional generation quality across different models for single-cell perturbation data. CaLMFlow (7b and (7c) generates data that accurately reflects the ground truth distribution across all combinatorial labels (cell type, perturbation, and chronicity), demonstrating its superior ability to understand complex conditions while maintaining a realistic overall data distribution. In contrast, methods like CFM-OT (7e), CFM-SB (7f), and scVI (7g) generate data where labels are blended, indicating an inability to comprehend conditions. While CFM (7d) and scGPT (7h) produce well-separated data, it's easily observed that this does not realistically represent the actual distribution when compared to the ground truth. For CaLMFlow, R.I. refers to randomly initialized CLM, and N.L. refers to natural language pretrained CLM.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing 2D embeddings or projections of single-cell data, with points colored by cell type and condition.\n\nVisible panels:\n- (a) Ground Truth\n- (b) CaLMFlow (R.I.)\n- (c) CaLMFlow (N.L.)\n- (d) CFM\n- (e) CFM-OT\n- (f) CFM-SB\n- (g) scVI\n- (h) scGPT\n- (i) CPA\n\nLegend/classes visible in panel (a):\n- B, IFNg, acute\n- CD4 T, IFNg, chronic\n- CD4 T, TNFa, acute\n- CD8 T, IFNa2, chronic\n- Monocyte, IL-6, acute\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell immune cell populations, including B cells, CD4 T cells, CD8 T cells, and monocytes.\n- Conditions involve cytokine/stimulation labels such as IFNg, TNFa, IFNa2, and IL-6, with acute or chronic status.\n\nModel/interface comparison:\n- The figure compares ground-truth cell-state structure against outputs from multiple computational models: CaLMFlow variants, CFM variants, scVI, scGPT, and CPA.\n- Each panel appears to visualize the same or related cell populations in a low-dimensional embedding, using colored point clouds.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Ground truth shows several separated clusters, including a large mixed red/orange/green cluster, a small blue cluster near the top, and a purple cluster separated on the right.\n- CaLMFlow variants preserve multi-cluster structure more visibly than several baselines, though layouts differ from ground truth.\n- CFM, scVI, and scGPT show more compressed or simplified structures.\n- CPA produces several elongated, separated trajectory-like colored structures rather than compact clusters."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 20, "caption": "Figure 8: Ablation results on number of time points. Left : CaLMFlow generated data from 8gaussians to 2moons, using different number of time points. Right : 2-Wasserstein and MMD performances as a function of number of time points. The plots show that increasing the number of time points improves the model performance.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing a 2D point-cloud/domain adaptation or trajectory-style experiment.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left side: six scatterplot panels arranged in a grid.\n  - Panel titles: `Ground Truth`, `Time points=6`, `Time points=8`, `Time points=10`, `Time points=12`, `Time points=15`.\n  - Legend in the first row indicates red points as `source` and blue points as `target`.\n  - Red source points form several separated clusters distributed around the plot.\n  - Blue target points form curved spiral or crescent-like distributions.\n  - Across increasing time points, the blue target distribution appears progressively transformed/aligned toward a broader curved manifold while red source clusters remain visible.\n\n- Right side: line plot with dual y-axes.\n  - X-axis: `Number of time points`.\n  - Left y-axis in red: `2-Wasserstein`.\n  - Right y-axis in blue: `MMD`.\n  - Red and blue curves both decrease as the number of time points increases, with small shaded uncertainty bands around the red curve.\n  - The plotted trend suggests improved distribution matching with more time points.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. The figure shows synthetic or abstract point clouds, not identifiable biological cells, tissues, molecules, organisms, microscopy images, or anatomical structures.\n\nModel/interface content:\n- No explicit model architecture or software interface is shown.\n- The figure visualizes transformation/alignment results and quantitative metrics.\n\nMain finding shown:\n- Increasing the number of time points appears to improve alignment between source and target distributions, reflected by decreasing `2-Wasserstein` and `MMD` values."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 20, "caption": "Figure 9: Uncurated conditional image generation comparison between CaLMFlow, CFM models, and DDPM on MNIST. CaLMFlow generates higher image quality images compared to both CFM variants and DDPM.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure comparing generated handwritten digit samples. It contains four side-by-side panels, each showing a grid of white digit-like characters on a black background.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- (a) CaLMFlow\n- (b) CFM\n- (c) CFM-OT\n- (c) DDPM, likely intended as a fourth panel label despite repeating “(c)”\n\nObjects shown:\n- Handwritten digit images resembling MNIST digits, arranged in rows and columns.\n- Digits include 0 through 9-like samples, with varying handwriting quality and distortion.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. This is not a biological microscopy, medical, or cellular image.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- The panels appear to compare outputs from different generative modeling methods: CaLMFlow, CFM, CFM-OT, and DDPM.\n- Each panel shows model-generated or reconstructed digit samples under the corresponding method.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- CaLMFlow samples appear cleaner and more consistently digit-like.\n- CFM, CFM-OT, and DDPM panels show more distorted or irregular digit shapes.\n- The figure visually supports a comparison of sample quality across generative models for handwritten digit generation."]}]

